 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S444-024\", \"clauses\": {\"read\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Read the fraction \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\). Which is the correct word name?\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 asks the learner to read a fraction and pick its word name, which is the Read task.\"}, \"write\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Write {fraction_words} in fraction notation.\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 asks the learner to write a word-named fraction in fraction notation, which is the Write task. Its word slot is nested F14 text.\"}, \"similar_fractions\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F17\", \"template\": \"{v}/{den}\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 holds the denominator fixed and moves the numerator one below or one above a candidate numerator, so its choices are fractions with the same denominator.\"}, \"fraction_notation\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"{num}/{den}\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 is the fraction notation form itself, used as keyed answer and as choices.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F05\", \"F06\", \"F07\", \"F10\", \"F12\", \"F13\", \"F14\", \"F15\", \"F16\", \"F17\", \"F19\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"How is \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) read in words?\", \"Read the fraction \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\). Which is the correct word name?\", \"So the {frac_lbl} is {num}/{den}.\", \"Which word name represents \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\)?\", \"{den} {get}ths\", \"{den}/{num}\", \"{get} {ordinal}\", \"{num}/{den}\", \"{num}/{other_d}\", \"{v}/{den}\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"The declared bound for this lc is fraction_type similar_proper, but several families can print a numerator greater than or equal to the denominator. F05 numerator members 1 to 7 are paired with denominator members 2 to 8, so improper fractions such as 7 over 2 can print. F06 and F10 numerators (1 to 4 and 1 to 7) pair with denominators 2 to 8, F07 prints numerator runs 1 to 4 with denominators 3 to 5 and 8, F15 and F16 pair numerators 1 to 7 with the denominator pool, and F17 pairs v (1 to 7) with the denominator pool. F13 and F19 are distractor families whose domains include improper values. The improper fraction concept is in NOT_YET_KNOWN, so these families load a concept beyond the lc's gates and beyond its declared bound. F12 template joins a denominator digit with a cardinal word plus the suffix ths; with cardinal words one, two, three and five the result is not a real word, and digits are mixed with words. F14 template joins a get slot (cardinal words or denominator-pool digits) with an ordinal slot (singular or plural from the list). Neither slot is bound to the other, so ungrammatical forms print, including plural agreement errors and the non-word plural of half, and digit forms. F19 members include digit-denominator forms with non-words, plural forms of half that are misspelled, and improper fractions. Families F01, F02, F03, F08, F09, F18 and the word-gate families use only in-vocabulary text and proper or correct values, so they do not fail on their own. F04 is generic and correct for proper fractions but is not a failing family on its own; it becomes misleading only when it appears beside an improper problem.\"}",
